---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Jewellness" description="Jewellness is a halo bracelet that tracks women's health without looking like a fitness tracker. Hridal's start-up project at Imperial.">
  <article class="case">
    <a class="case-back" href="/projects">All projects</a>
    <p class="case-kind">Start-up, Imperial Entrepreneurship module, October 2026 to now</p>
    <h1>Jewellness</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">A halo bracelet that tracks sleep, heart health and your cycle, with every sensor hidden in the clasp, so it looks like jewellery and never like a fitness tracker.</p>

    <figure class="case-viewer">
      <div class="viewer-box" id="viewer">
        <img src="/projects/jewellness/product.jpg" alt="The Jewellness bracelet and its features" />
        <button class="btn solid" id="load-viewer">Open the 3D model</button>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The 3D model lets you turn the bracelet, change the metal and stones, and look at the clasp from the skin side. <a href="/projects/jewellness/viewer.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it full screen</a></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Idea, customer research, market sizing and financial model</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Interviews</dt><dd>30+ potential customers</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>Fitness trackers only help if people actually wear them, and a lot of people stop. Around 42% lose interest within six months, 29% of people in the UK find them unattractive, and almost half are worried about what happens to their data. I noticed the same gap myself, because I wanted something that did what a tracker does without looking like one, and I could not find it.</p>
    <img class="case-slide" src="/projects/jewellness/problem.jpg" alt="Slide showing why people stop wearing fitness trackers" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>What I found</h2>
    <p>I spoke to more than 30 people, and the answers pointed the same way. Seven in ten were uncomfortable with how a smartwatch or obvious tracker looks, every single one would value a device with no subscription, and 62% named sleep tracking as the thing they cared about most.</p>

    <h2>The product</h2>
    <p>The bracelet keeps every electronic part inside the clasp, which sits on the underside of the wrist where the sensors can stay against the skin. The links on top are ordinary halo links with no electronics in them, so it moves like a normal tennis bracelet. It has a two-week battery, no subscription, a tap-for-help alert, and all the health data stays on your phone.</p>
    <img class="case-slide" src="/projects/jewellness/product.jpg" alt="Slide introducing the bracelet's features" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>The market</h2>
    <p>I sized the market starting from the UK wearables market of about $5.9bn, narrowed it to the roughly $1.8bn spent on health and fitness wearables by women, and modelled what reaching 1% of that within five years would look like, which comes to around $18m a year.</p>
    <img class="case-slide" src="/projects/jewellness/market.jpg" alt="Slide with the market sizing" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>Where it is now</h2>
    <p>I am now testing demand with a low-cost pretotype before building anything expensive, because the whole idea depends on people actually wanting to wear it every day.</p>
  </article>
  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('load-viewer');
    btn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const box = document.getElementById('viewer')!;
      const f = document.createElement('iframe');
      f.src = '/projects/jewellness/viewer.html'; f.title = '3D model of the Jewellness bracelet'; f.loading = 'lazy';
      f.setAttribute('allow', 'fullscreen');
      box.replaceChildren(f);
    });
  </script>
</Base>
